// A mapped stdlib extern that FAULTS.
//
// `String.repeat` with a negative count is a runtime error. A builtin's own
// text names no line, so the report puts the line of the program's call in
// front of it, as for any trap inside the stdlib, and the words after it
// reach the user VERBATIM. So the text is a contract rt owns, and
// the `strings.String.repeat:` qualification (which `Int.shift_left`'s does NOT
// share) is part of it rather than something to tidy.
//
// The print before the fault is the other half of the fixture: stdout must carry
// `before` before the error reaches stderr, which is what rt.Print
// writing unbuffered straight to fd 1 buys.
import std/io

fn main() {
    io.print("before")
    io.print(String.repeat("ab", 0 - 2))
}
